module gunbc.stage0_emitted_population_manifest

import std.types { String }

// THE NAME OF THE ARTIFACT IN WHICH THE EMITTER DECLARES THE POPULATION IT PRODUCED.
//
// This module exists to hold ONE string, and that is the point rather than an accident of
// factoring. The manifest has two ends -- `v1.compiler.emit_rust` writes it and
// `gunbc.stage0_rust_host_observation` reads it -- and if each spelled the basename itself,
// the artifact would have two names for one file, which is the nicknaming failure DESIGN 3
// names and the exact class the manifest exists to end. A shared leaf module is the smallest
// carrier that lets both ends resolve the same symbol without the reader importing the whole
// emitter's closure to get at one literal.
//
// ONLY THE BASENAME IS SHARED, DELIBERATELY. The two ends compose different roots because they
// address different spaces: the emitter writes target-relative (under `rust_source_root()`,
// i.e. `src/`), the observation addresses repository-relative (under `stage0_package_root`,
// i.e. `src/v1/stage0/`). Hoisting a single "path" here would force one of them to hold a path
// in a space it does not speak, so the shared fact is the part that is genuinely one fact.
data emitted_population_manifest_basename: String = "emitted_population.rs"

// The manifest's line grammar, stated once for the same reason the basename is.
//
// The emitter renders one line per path as `line_prefix ++ path`, joined by the separator; the
// observation recovers the population by splitting on the separator and stripping the prefix.
// One grammar read in both directions (DESIGN 4) -- the structural inverse, not a second parser.
//
// WHY THE ARTIFACT IS A `.rs` OF COMMENT LINES, WHICH LOOKS ODD UNTIL YOU SEE WHAT IT BUYS.
// Two demands pull opposite ways. GATING wants the manifest inside the population
// `required_regen` already compares, and that population is `.rs`-shaped end to end: every
// compared member is rustfmt-normalized before its digest is taken, so a `.txt` member is
// REFUSED at normalization ("expected one of `!` or `::`, found `.`") -- measured, not
// predicted. READING wants recovery without parsing Rust, because a text-parse standing in for
// a modeled fact is exactly what this lane spent the day deleting (gunbc#8690, cssl_assemble's
// seed lib.rs oracle), and a generated `const &[&str]` would put one right back.
//
// A file of nothing but `//` lines satisfies both. It is valid Rust, so it enters the compared
// population by construction and normalizes to itself (a comments-only file is already a
// rustfmt fixed point, which is the property DESIGN's two-consumer rule demands). And it is
// read by splitting and stripping a literal prefix -- no Rust grammar is involved on the
// reading side at all. It is deliberately NOT a module: it is appended to the emitted file list
// AFTER `lib.rs` is rendered, so no `pub mod` line names it and nothing compiles it.
// DISSOLUTION: this shape is owed to the compared population being Rust-shaped end to end, not
// to anything about the fact itself. When that population becomes medium-agnostic -- when
// `required_regen`'s comparison normalizes an artifact by its declared medium rather than by
// assuming Rust -- the manifest becomes the line-delimited text it wants to be, this paragraph
// deletes, and the `.rs` extension goes with it. Until then the extension is load-bearing and
// "cleaning it up" into a `const &[&str]` would silently reintroduce a Rust parse on the read
// side, which is the class gunbc#8690 deleted.
data emitted_population_manifest_line_prefix: String = "// "

data emitted_population_manifest_line_separator: String = "\n"

// THE SECOND ARTIFACT IN THIS CHANNEL: THE EXPORT SURFACE THE EMITTER DECIDED PER MODULE.
//
// The population manifest above answers WHICH FILES the emit produced. It does not answer what
// any of them EXPORTS, and that is a different fact with a different consumer: a seed installs
// one artifact per module, so a module whose linkage surface depends on which entry requested
// it is not installable, and deciding that requires the export set rather than the path set.
//
// The emitter already DECIDES that set -- `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_module_parts` holds the
// module's rendered `pub use` lines and its authored declaration list as values, one step before
// it joins them into text -- and until now it discarded it. Every consumer that wanted it had to
// go back to the emitted `.rs` and parse Rust, which is the class gunbc#8690 deleted and which
// the paragraph above rules against for this channel by name.
//
// SAME PREFIX, SAME SEPARATOR, SAME REASONING. This artifact reuses the two rows above rather
// than spelling its own, because "a line is `// ` ++ payload, joined by newline" is one fact and
// a second spelling of it would be the nicknaming failure this module was created to end. Only
// the basename and the field grammar are new, and they are new because they are genuinely new
// facts. The `.rs` extension is load-bearing for the same measured reason and dissolves under the
// same condition as the population manifest's.
data emitted_export_surface_manifest_basename: String = "emitted_export_surface.rs"

// Within one line the fields are separated by a single space, which is total for this payload by
// construction rather than by convention: every field is a Rust module filename or a Rust
// identifier, and neither can contain a space.
//
// A row is `source_module ++ FS ++ target_filename ++ FS ++ tag ++ (FS ++ member)*`, so splitting
// on the separator yields the module identity, its file locator, the row kind, and the members
// with no further grammar. One grammar read in both directions (DESIGN 4): the emitter joins, the
// reader splits, and there is no second parser.
//
// THE IDENTITY AND THE LOCATOR ARE BOTH CARRIED because neither determines the other.
// `module_to_filename` is `split(".") |> join("_")` and is many-to-one -- `a.b_c` and `a_b.c` both
// render `a_b_c` -- so a consumer joining on the filename would merge two distinct modules into
// one row and refuse on the merge, a false positive it could not tell from a real finding.
data emitted_export_surface_manifest_field_separator: String = " "

// ONE MODULE ENVELOPE AND TWO SURFACE PROJECTIONS, ALWAYS.
//
// That is not padding. A consumer asking "does this module reexport anything" must be able to
// distinguish DECLARED EMPTY from NOT OBSERVED. The envelope makes module population explicit;
// the always-present projection rows make each empty member set explicit. Neither must be inferred
// from the other.
//
// `reexport` is the LINKAGE SURFACE: the names this module's emitted `pub use` lines re-export,
// which is what varies under closure and what a seed cannot install two of. `decl` is the
// module's own authored top-level declarations, in the emitter's rendering order, which is the
// content-identity axis beside it: two observations may agree on declarations and disagree on
// linkage, and that pair is exactly the distinction the consuming census is built on.
// The envelope row is the module-population fact. The two surface rows below are projections of
// that record; neither is allowed to stand in for its existence. Keeping this tag in the shared
// grammar makes the producer's one-record-per-module value survive the text boundary instead of
// forcing every reader to recover the population by unioning whichever projections it happens to
// consume.
data emitted_export_surface_module_tag: String = "module"

data emitted_export_surface_reexport_tag: String = "reexport"

data emitted_export_surface_declaration_tag: String = "decl"
